# Vanessa Automation Scenario Policy

> RULES — mandatory policies for Vanessa Automation scenario UI tests.

## Purpose

This rule fixes what an agent is allowed to turn into a `.feature` scenario and when it should do so.

Vanessa scenario tests are written not "for example" but as a formalization of a real project requirement.

## Trigger conditions

The rule applies when an agent:

- writes a new `.feature` scenario;
- extends an existing Vanessa Automation scenario;
- chooses which project case to use for the next smoke/regression test.

## Requirements

### MUST

| Requirement | Description |
|------------|----------|
| Real source of the requirement | The scenario MUST rely either on the specification of a new task or on an existing business case/fixed project task |
| No imaginary cases | You must not invent scenarios without a project requirement source |
| One scenario — one behavior | Every new scenario verifies one observable behavior of the form or business flow |
| Existing steps first | Before adding a new step, the agent MUST look for an existing step in the Vanessa library and the project scenarios |
| First scenario — short smoke | For a new case, the first scenario MUST be short, stable, and suitable for smoke runs |

### SHOULD

| Requirement | Description |
|------------|----------|
| Explicit source | The comment/context SHOULD specify the source: specification, task number, or the case under validation |
| Separate data setup | Data setup SHOULD be moved outside of the main scenario if it is not a key part of the behavior |
| Observable expectation | The expectation SHOULD be expressed through the UI state, available requisites, and visible behavior rather than internal technical details |

## Correct formulation of the scenario source

Use the following interpretation:

- For a new task — the scenario is built on an approved specification and describes the expected behavior before or alongside implementation.
- For an already implemented task — the scenario is built on a real working case and locks in the regression.

Incorrect: "take only completed tasks."

Correct: "use only real project requirements: either the specification of a new task or an existing business case/fixed task."

## Antipatterns

| Antipattern | Why it is forbidden |
|-------------|---------------|
| Invented document/flow without a project source | The scenario does not protect a real requirement |
| Long scenario spanning 5-7 meanings | It fails unstably and is hard to diagnose |
| Checking internal details instead of behavior | The scenario is fragile and poorly matches the user contract |

